      "goal": "Spectral investigation: determine the exact Dirichlet eigenvalues and eigenvectors, prove optimality and equality classification, and develop a sharp quantitative stability theorem for near extremizers.",
      "relative_weight": 0.55,
      "initial_prompt": "ROUND 1 OF A PLANNED THREE-ROUND SPECTRAL INVESTIGATION.\n\nAssumptions: N is an arbitrary integer N >= 2. The variables x_0,...,x_N are real, with x_0 = x_N = 0. Define S(x) = sum_{j=1}^{N-1} x_j^2 and E(x) = sum_{j=0}^{N-1} (x_{j+1}-x_j)^2.\n\nObjective for this round: Derive rigorously the optimal constant C_N in S(x) <= C_N E(x), using the Dirichlet discrete Laplacian on the N-1 interior vertices. Construct its matrix, derive its eigenvalues and eigenvectors directly from the finite-difference equation, and justify completeness and orthogonality. Identify the smallest eigenvalue, prove sharpness, and characterize every equality case, including the zero sequence. Treat N = 2 explicitly rather than assuming multiple eigenvalues exist.\n\nSpecific proof requirements: Derive rather than merely quote the sine eigenvector formula. Verify the difference equation and both Dirichlet boundary conditions. Establish that the eigenvectors form a basis, with all relevant eigenvalues distinct. Connect the quadratic form E(x) to the Laplacian and use the Rayleigh quotient to prove the exact inequality. Demonstrate attainment and uniqueness of nonzero extremizing directions. Record the spectral gap lambda_2-lambda_1 for N >= 3 as a candidate ingredient for Round 2, but do not substitute an unproved stability claim for this round's required proofs.\n\nPlanned subsequent rounds: Round 2 will establish a normalized quantitative stability theorem using the spectral gap, including exact deficit-to-distance constants. Round 3 will determine which stability constants are optimal, examine near-extremizer constructions, and audit N = 2, N = 3, and normalization degeneracies. Do not execute those later rounds now.\n\nReport requirements: Return one concise complete report, preferably below 1200 words, containing assumptions, conclusions, mathematical evidence with derivations, any obstacles or unresolved points, and concrete recommendations for Round 2. Distinguish established results from conjectures. Do not use external data or execute software. Do not claim independent verification. Stop immediately after this single research round."
    },
    {
      "goal": "Elementary finite-sum investigation: establish sharpness through an independent algebraic proof, verify all endpoint terms and exceptional cases, and develop elementary stability bounds without relying on spectral decomposition.",
      "relative_weight": 0.45,
      "initial_prompt": "ROUND 1 OF A PLANNED THREE-ROUND ELEMENTARY INVESTIGATION.\n\nAssumptions: N is an arbitrary integer N >= 2, and x_0,...,x_N are real with x_0 = x_N = 0. Write S(x) = sum_{j=1}^{N-1} x_j^2 and E(x) = sum_{j=0}^{N-1} (x_{j+1}-x_j)^2.\n\nObjective for this round: Find a rigorous elementary finite-sum proof of the optimal inequality S(x) <= C_N E(x), establish sharpness independently of a general matrix eigenvalue theorem, characterize equality, and verify the treatment of both boundaries and all exceptional small-N cases.\n\nSuggested concrete approach to investigate: Define w_j = sin(pi*j/N), positive for 1 <= j <= N-1 and zero at both endpoints. From elementary trigonometric identities, derive the second-difference relation for w. Attempt to prove the exact ground-state-transform identity\n\nE(x) - 4 sin^2(pi/(2*N)) S(x) = sum_{j=1}^{N-2} w_j*w_{j+1}*(x_{j+1}/w_{j+1}-x_j/w_j)^2.\n\nDo not assume this identity without proving it. Expand both sides and account explicitly for the endpoint edges (0,1) and (N-1,N). Avoid undefined ratios at j = 0 and j = N. Explain how positivity proves the proposed sharp constant, and separately verify sharpness by substituting the explicit sine sequence into the original finite sums. Determine every equality case, including N = 2, for which the displayed sum on the right is empty.\n\nIf the proposed transform cannot be established, pursue another elementary summation, completion-of-squares, or weighted Cauchy-Schwarz proof. Report any gap accurately rather than asserting the identity without justification.\n\nPlanned subsequent rounds: Round 2 will construct a quantitative stability estimate using only elementary weighted finite sums, telescoping, and Cauchy-Schwarz. One possible route is to set y_j = x_j/w_j and estimate its weighted variance by its weighted neighboring differences. Round 3 will audit the constants and all boundary conventions, compare the result with the spectral approach, and analyze possible equality or degeneracy in the stability estimate. Do not carry out those later rounds now.\n\nReport requirements: Produce one concise complete report, preferably below 1200 words, including assumptions, explicit algebraic derivations, equality and sharpness arguments, edge-case verification, obstacles if any, and specific next steps for Round 2. Clearly separate proof from conjecture. Do not use external data or execute software. Do not claim independent verification. Stop after this one round."
    }
  ]
