lved statement. Each round returns one concise complete report; aim below 1200 words while retaining essential proofs. Do not require external data or software execution. All experimental leaves actually use GPT-6 Extra High; never claim Pro or independent verification.", "inbound_internal_messages": [], "kind": "parent", "model_roles": {"child": "GPT-6 Extra High", "parent": "GPT-6 Extra High", "production_leaf_default": "GPT-6 Pro", "production_parent_default": "GPT-6 Extra High"}, "node_id": "root", "report": {"evidence": "1. Quadratic form and matrix construction.\n\nLet y = (x_1,...,x_{N\u22121})\u1d40, with x_0 = x_N = 0. Define (Ay)_j = 2x_j\u2212x_{j\u22121}\u2212x_{j+1}, for 1 <= j <= N\u22121. Thus A has diagonal entries 2 and nearest-neighbor entries \u22121. Expanding the squares and collecting adjacent cross terms gives\n\nE(x) = \u03a3_{j=0}^{N\u22121}(x_{j+1}\u2212x_j)\u00b2 = 2\u03a3_{j=1}^{N\u22121}x_j\u00b2\u22122\u03a3_{j=1}^{N\u22122}x_jx_{j+1} = y\u1d40Ay.\n\nAlso S(x) = y\u1d40y. For nonzero y, E(x) > 0 because E(x) = 0 would force every consecutive difference to vanish and hence x_j = x_0 = 0 for every j. Furthermore,\n\n4S(x)\u2212E(x) = \u03a3_{j=0}^{N\u22121}(x_{j+1}+x_j)\u00b2 > 0\n\nfor nonzero y: vanishing of every sum, together with x_0 = 0, would again force all entries to vanish. Consequently, every eigenvalue \u03bb of the real symmetric matrix A satisfies 0 < \u03bb < 4.\n\n2. Direct solution of the eigenvalue equation.\n\nThe equation Av = \u03bbv becomes\n\nv_{j+1}\u2212(2\u2212\u03bb)v_j+v_{j\u22121} = 0, 1 <= j <= N\u22121,\n\nwith v_0 = v_N = 0. Since 0 < \u03bb < 4, there exists a unique \u03b8 in (0,\u03c0) such that 2\u2212\u03bb = 2cos \u03b8. The characteristic equation is r\u00b2\u22122(cos \u03b8)r+1 = 0, whose roots are exp(i\u03b8) and exp(\u2212i\u03b8). Every real solution of the recurrence therefore has the form\n\nv_j = a cos(j\u03b8)+b sin(j\u03b8).\n\nThe first boundary condition v_0 = 0 gives a = 0. A nonzero eigenvector requires b \u2260 0. The second boundary condition v_N = 0 consequently implies sin(N\u03b8) = 0. Since 0 < \u03b8 < \u03c0, necessarily \u03b8 = k\u03c0/N for a unique integer k in {1,...,N\u22121}. Therefore\n\n\u03bb_k = 2\u22122cos(k\u03c0/N) = 4sin\u00b2(k\u03c0/(2N)),\n\nv_j^(k) = sin(jk\u03c0/N).\n\nConversely, these formulas satisfy both boundary conditions. The identity sin((j+1)\u03b8)+sin((j\u22121)\u03b8) = 2cos \u03b8 sin(j\u03b8) verifies the finite-difference equation directly. Each constructed eigenvector is nonzero because v_1^(k) = sin(k\u03c0/N) \u2260 0.\n\n3. Distinctness, orthogonality, and completeness.\n\nThe cosine function is strictly decreasing on (0,\u03c0), so\n\n0 < \u03bb_1 < \u03bb_2 < ... < \u03bb_{N\u22121} < 4.\n\nFor k \u2260 \u2113, symmetry of A gives\n\n\u03bb_k \u27e8v^(k),v^(\u2113)\u27e9 = \u27e8Av^(k),v^(\u2113)\u27e9 = \u27e8v^(k),Av^(\u2113)\u27e9 = \u03bb_\u2113 \u27e8v^(k),v^(\u2113)\u27e9.\n\nSince \u03bb_k \u2260 \u03bb_\u2113, the inner product vanishes. Thus the N\u22121 nonzero eigenvectors are mutually orthogonal in the (N\u22121)-dimensional space and necessarily form a basis; no additional eigenvalues or eigenvector directions exist.\n\nTheir squared norms equal N/2. Indeed, using sin\u00b2 t = (1\u2212cos(2t))/2 and the geometric-series identity \u03a3_{j=0}^{N\u22121} exp(2\u03c0ikj/N) = 0 for 1 <= k <= N\u22121, we obtain\n\n\u03a3_{j=1}^{N\u22121} sin\u00b2(jk\u03c0/N) = N/2.\n\nTherefore \u03c6^(k) = sqrt(2/N)v^(k) is an orthonormal eigenbasis.\n\n4. Optimal inequality, attainment, and equality classification.\n\nExpand an arbitrary admissible interior vector as y = \u03a3_{k=1}^{N\u22121} a_k \u03c6^(k). Orthogonality and the eigenvalue equations yield\n\nS(x) = \u03a3_{k=1}^{N\u22121} a_k\u00b2,\n\nE(x) = \u03a3_{k=1}^{N\u22121} \u03bb_k a_k\u00b2.\n\nIt follows that\n\nE(x)\u2212\u03bb_1 S(x) = \u03a3_{k=2}^{N\u22121}(\u03bb_k\u2212\u03bb_1)a_k\u00b2 >= 0,\n\nwhere an empty sum is interpreted a